  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "An FHA loan is a government-backed mortgage insured by the Federal Housing Administration, designed for low-to-moderate-income borrowers, offering accessible home financing with down payments as low as 3.5% and lower credit score requirements (typically 580+). These loans are ideal for first-time buyers, allowing flexible income guidelines and lenient debt-to-income ratios. Key Aspects of FHA Loans Down Payment: 3.5% for credit scores of 580 or higher; 10% for scores between 500-579. Mortgage Insurance Premium (MIP): Requires an upfront fee (1.75%) and annual premiums (0.45% to 1.05%) for the life of the loan, or 11 years if a 10% down payment is made. Property Requirements: Must be a primary residence and pass a stricter appraisal for health and safety. Loan Limits (2026): Ranging from a floor of $541,287 in lower-cost areas to a ceiling of $1,249,125 for high-cost areas. Bankruptcy/Foreclosure: Generally allows applicants 2 years post-Chapter 7 bankruptcy and 3 years post-foreclosure. Pros and Cons Pros: Low down payment, flexible credit/income requirements, competitive rates, and assumable mortgages. Cons: Mandatory, long-term mortgage insurance; strict property appraisal standards; loan limits based on location. Common FHA Loan Types Fixed-Rate Mortgage: Standard purchase loan. FHA 203(k) Loan: For purchasing and rehabilitating/renovating a home. HECM (Reverse Mortgage): For seniors 62+. Energy Efficient Mortgage: To add energy-saving improvements. Streamline Refinance: Simple refinancing for existing FHA loans.",
